<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amoghapasa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Amoghapāśa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amoghapasa is a form of the bodhisattva Avalokiteshvara in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vajrayana">Vajrayana</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, whose name refers to his unfailing noose for saving beings. He was especially honoured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> in the 13th and 14th centuries. A famous statue of him, sent in 1286 by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kertanegara">Kertanegara</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singhasari">Singhasari</a> to Dharmasraya in Sumatra, bears the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padang-roco-inscription">Padang Roco inscription</a> and a later inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adityawarman">Adityawarman</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amoghapasa.jpg" alt="Amoghapasa" width="320" height="508" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hyougushi · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khasarpana_Lokesvara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
